<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_magnetic-flux" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Magnetic Flux</title>

	<p>The definition you saw for <xref ref="def_flux"/> can be extended to define a new quantity called <em>magnetic flux</em>.</p>
		
		<!--
    <video youtube="qMJK_sjdUCw"><description>DESCRIPTION</description></video>
	-->
	
	<definition>
		<title>Magnetic flux</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p><term>Magnetic flux</term> is a measure of how much magnetic field is passing perpendicularly through a surface: <me>\mathit{\Phi}_{B} = \iint \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{A}</me></p>
			<p>The double-integral sign indicates that the integral is over a two-dimensional surface, and the limits of integration must be chosen so that they cover the entirety of the desired surface.  The differential area vector <m>d\vec{A}</m> represents an infinitesimal piece of the total surface.</p>
		</statement>
	</definition>

<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		
		<activity>
      <title>Flux through a Book</title>
	  <introduction>
      <p></p>
			<p>Suppose you have a region of space where the magnetic field is uniform and points entirely in the <m>y</m>-direction with magnitude <m>B_1</m>.</p>
				</introduction>
				<task>
					<statement>
						<p>
							Make a quick sketch of the magnetic field, including <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>-, and <m>z</m>-axes.
						</p>
					</statement>
				</task>
				<task>
					<statement>
						<p>
							Add the following two surfaces to your sketch:
						</p>
						<ul>
							<li>
								<p>
									<alert>Surface 1:</alert> A circle with radius <m>R</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
								</p>
							</li>
							<li>
								<p>
									<alert>Surface 2:</alert> A circle with radius <m>R</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane.
								</p>
							</li>
							</ul>
					</statement>
				</task>
				<task>
					<statement>
						<p>
							Sketch an area vector for each surface.
						</p>
					</statement>
				</task>
				<task>
					<statement>
						<p>
							Use your chosen area vectors and the given magnetic field to calculate the magnetic flux through each surface.
						</p>
					</statement>
				</task>
		</activity>
		
	</subsubsection>

<!--NO AI-->
</section>

